      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "The elongated styloid process has been noted in medical literature for hundreds of years and is commonly referred to as Eagle's Syndrome. The presence of anomalous structures is always a concern to therapists and surgeons. The anatomy, embryology, etiology, plus treatment risks are reviewed. The affects of hyperflexion/hyperextension (whiplash) injuries are exacerbated in the presence of this unexpected calcified structure. The clinical significance of Eagle's Syndrome in the treatment of the trauma patient is explored."},
